<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>CFBundleName</key>
    <string>Agterm Remote</string>
    <!-- The file inside Contents/MacOS. Without it the bundle has no executable to launch. -->
    <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
    <string>AgtermRemote</string>
    <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
    <string>dev.isachivka.agtermremote</string>
    <!--
      The icon, WITHOUT its extension, which is what CFBundleIconFile means. bundle.sh generates
      AgtermRemote.icns from Mark.svg and copies it into Contents/Resources before signing, and then
      asserts that this key and that file agree: a bundle declaring an icon it does not carry is a
      lie the system tells the owner in the Finder, and it is worse than declaring none.

      The app is LSUIElement, so this is seen in the Finder, in Login Items, and in the Privacy and
      Security panel - not in the Dock, where an accessory app has no tile. Those three places are
      exactly where somebody goes to work out what is running on their Mac and whether to trust it,
      which is why an app that asks for a network listener cannot show up there as a blank page.
    -->
    <key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
    <string>AgtermRemote</string>
    <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
    <string>APPL</string>
    <!--
      No Dock icon and no window at launch. This is the half macOS reads when the app is launched as a
      bundle; main.swift also calls setActivationPolicy(.accessory) for the `swift run` path, so a
      developer run behaves the same way as a real one.
    -->
    <key>LSUIElement</key>
    <true/>
    <!--
      Overwritten at release time by scripts/bundle.sh, from the same version string the .apk is
      stamped with. One place decides what a release is called; a literal here that anybody edits by
      hand is a second source of truth, and the two halves of a paired product must never be able to
      disagree about which release they belong to.
    -->
    <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
    <string>0.0.0</string>
</dict>
</plist>
